{"causes":["The Brothers Grimm's collection of Germanic folktales created a platform for stories like Rumplestiltskin to gain widespread recognition.","The rise of urbanization and industrialization during the 19th century led to an increased interest in folklore and fairy tales as escapism from the harsh realities of city life.","The Brothers Grimm's publication of their collection in 1812 marked a significant turning point in the popularization of European folklore, paving the way for future adaptations and reinterpretations."],"effects":["The Disney adaptation of Rumplestiltskin in 1955 introduced the character to a broader audience, cementing its place in popular culture.","Rumplestiltskin's influence can be seen in modern interpretations of Germanic folklore, such as the Netflix series 'Grimm', which has helped to revitalize interest in these stories among a new generation.","The tale's exploration of themes such as deception and power continues to resonate with audiences today, making it a timeless classic."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Brothers-Grimm","sourceName":"The Brothers Grimm","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"Rumplestiltskin","targetName":"Rumplestiltskin","context":"Published their collection of Germanic folktales in 1812."},{"sourceSlug":"Industrialization","sourceName":"Urbanization and Industrialization","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Rumplestiltskin","targetName":"Rumplestiltskin","context":"Led to an increased interest in folklore and fairy tales as escapism from the harsh realities of city life."},{"sourceSlug":"Disney-Adaptation","sourceName":"The Disney Company","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Rumplestiltskin","targetName":"Rumplestiltskin","context":"Published the 1955 Disney adaptation of Rumplestiltskin."},{"sourceSlug":"Netflix-Series","sourceName":"The Netflix Company","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Grimm","targetName":"Grimm (TV series)","context":"Has helped to revitalize interest in Germanic folklore and fairy tales among a new generation."},{"sourceSlug":"Germanic-Folklore","sourceName":"Germanic Folklore","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Rumplestiltskin","targetName":"Rumplestiltskin","context":"Is part of the larger corpus of Germanic folktales and fairy tales."},{"sourceSlug":"Grimm-Series","sourceName":"The Grimm Company","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"Netflix-Series","targetName":"Grimm (TV series)","context":"Has been adapted into the Netflix series 'Grimm', further increasing its visibility and appeal."},{"sourceSlug":"Folklore-Corpus","sourceName":"The Folklore Corpus","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Rumplestiltskin","targetName":"Rumplestiltskin","context":"Is part of the larger corpus of European folklore and fairy tales."},{"sourceSlug":"20th-Century-Adaptations","sourceName":"The 20th Century","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Rumplestiltskin","targetName":"Rumplestiltskin","context":"Has had a significant impact on the development of modern adaptations and reinterpretations."}],"places":[{"name":"Frankfurt, Germany","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Berlin, Germany","role":"Publication site"},{"name":"Hamburg, Germany","role":"Inspirational location"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Rumplestiltskin","type":"Reference article","slug":"rumplestiltskin"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q322188","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumpelstiltskin"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Rumplestiltskin contributed to the historical record of the Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Rumpelstiltskin (German: Rumpelstilzchen) is a German fairy tale first recorded by the Brothers Grimm in Kinder- und Hausmärchen ('Children's and Household Tales') as tale KHM 55, in the first edition (1812), and revised in subsequent editions with the most widely read version appearing in the 7th edition (1857). The tale narrates the story of a miller who boasts to the king that his daughter can spin gold from straw — the daughter is locked in a room with a spinning wheel and straw and told to spin gold or die; a mysterious little man appears and spins the gold in exchange for her necklace, ring, and finally the promise of her firstborn child; when the king marries her and the firstborn arrives, the little man returns to claim the child; but the queen is granted a three-day reprieve if she can guess his name, and ultimately discovers his name (Rumpelstiltskin) through a spy who observes him dancing and singing in the forest.

Rumpelstiltskin is classified as ATU type 500 ('The Name of the Supernatural Helper') and exists in related versions across European and world folk traditions — the motif of the supernatural helper whose power is broken when his name is discovered is widespread in Germanic, British (Tom Tit Tot), and Scottish folk traditions. The tale's central mystery — the power of a secret name — is connected to the ancient magical belief that knowing a being's true name gives power over it, a belief found in Egyptian, Hebrew, Greek, and Norse magical traditions.

Rumpelstiltskin's cultural legacy in the modern era is remarkably varied — the name 'Rumpelstiltskin' has become an idiom in some legal contexts (notably in Rumpelstiltskin-related constitutional cases in the United States about the right to know one's biological father's name), the tale is a central text in feminist analysis (the miller's lie commodifies his daughter; the queen is dependent on supernatural help to spin gold she cannot actually spin), and it is one of the most frequently adapted of all Grimm fairy tales in popular fiction.","subjects":["German Literature","19th Century","Fairy Tale","Brothers Grimm","Folk Literature","Magical Thinking","Feminist Criticism","Children's Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Rumplestiltskin's enduring cultural footprint is a testament to the power of folklore and fairy tales in shaping our collective imagination.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
